NANO(1)                       Funny CLI Manual                       NANO(1)

NAME
    nano — Open a file in the terminal editor using the familiar
           Unix-style editor name, without actually launching GNU nano.

SYNOPSIS
    nano <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'nano' command is an alternate name for the CLI's default
    'edit' command.

    It does not launch the actual GNU nano editor. Instead, it passes
    all arguments directly to 'edit', which creates and runs the
    application's TerminalEditor.

    In other words:

        nano <file>

    is effectively:

        edit <file>

    'edit' is the canonical command. 'nano' exists for users who
    naturally reach for the familiar Unix editor name.

EDITOR SELECTION
    edit
        The default and canonical command for opening the built-in
        TerminalEditor.

    nano
        A familiar Unix-style name for the same built-in editor.
        It does NOT launch GNU nano.

    vim
        A Vim-style name for the same built-in editor.
        It does NOT launch Vim.

    vi
        A classic vi-style name for the same built-in editor.
        It does NOT launch vi.

    All four commands ultimately use the same TerminalEditor.

ARGUMENTS
    file
        The file to open in the terminal editor.

        Required. If omitted, the underlying 'edit' command prints
        its usage message.

BEHAVIOR
    All arguments received by 'nano' are passed directly to:

        self.cmd_edit(args)

    This means 'nano' inherits the path handling, editor behavior,
    and error reporting of 'edit'.

    Think of 'nano' as 'edit' wearing a familiar Unix nametag.

EXAMPLES
    > edit README.md
        The canonical way to open README.md.

    > nano README.md
        Opens README.md in the same TerminalEditor.

        You typed 'nano'. The computer heard 'edit'.
        Everybody wins.

FUN FACTS
    - 'edit' is the default editor command.
    - 'nano' is provided as a familiar alternative command name.
    - GNU nano is not launched.
    - The actual editor implementation is TerminalEditor.
    - File completion is enabled.
    - The command delegates directly to 'edit'.

